rota lap 4511, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; law and boundary. Single-Stranded is what named this lap's repair: the elder scan braided two questions into one verdict, and the recovered one separates them into gated, reported, and unresolved, so either can be drawn out and read alone.
think The card named the next lap outright: stash@{2} holds the self_matching_assert family whole, and stash_record gated at unlanded=2. Two laps had already proven this package, one TESTED and one GREEN, and fleet_round_open stashed both before either could send.
think Restored with git checkout stash@{2} -- <paths> rather than git stash pop, so the tree object is read and the 100755 mode on both fixtures survives. A blob read lands 100644, which the exec-bit law records.
think Re-proven on metal rather than read off the parked logs. Control 177 checks, zero failures, 16.4s. Witness exit 0. Scan gated=0 ceiling=0 over 2,456 runners and 17,043 asserts -- the corpus grew from the parked reading's 2,447 and 16,812, so the gate holds on today's tree rather than on the day it was written.
think The card, the ledger, the roster and the shelf index had all moved since the stash base, so those four edits were re-derived by hand against HEAD 6141be50f4 rather than applied from the stash.
obs The repair itself: the elder gated by the utility's NAME, so stat was self-matching whatever its options. stat --format=%s path prints the size alone and names no operand; basename and dirname were labelled the same way. The recovered reader admits exactly two quoted tokens, so a three-token run fails the admit and reports rather than gates.
obs An error of my own, and it is the same one the recovered log records against its own author. I launched the cold roster pass detached and then restored six files under it. The card's directive says hold still while it runs. TERMed by recorded pid through fleet_call --pid -- the bounded form, never a name pattern -- and relaunched over the settled tree.
obs Fetch-before-book: git fetch xy, then reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next answered 684. The parked booking of %664 had been spent by peers while it sat, so the row derives above. The key is the stamp, 20260909.084248, which is what the row carries in its own head.
obs REDS stood at 39,994 of 40,960. The row was drafted at 1,298 bytes and tightened to 955 rather than folding a CLOSED row to free room, since a shelf name is a collision surface and a peer took one from me six minutes wide two laps ago. The pin now reads 40,949 -- 11 bytes free.
obs Two logs landing on a CLOSED shelf is a question the law leaves open. I added both rows, in descending stamp order, because a shelf that omits a log it holds has stopped pointing. session_roster_agree then read stale=2 and both rosters were corrected off the shelf's own rows: 20260909 moved 105 to 107.
